      summaryEn: "Completed after 27 years of field investigation and three revisions between 1552 and 1578, the Compendium of Materia Medica (Bencao Gangmu) by naturalist Li Shizhen (1518–1593, Qichun, Hubei) was first woodblock-printed in Nanjing in 1596 and inscribed on the UNESCO Memory of the World Register in 2011. Across 52 chapters, 1.9 million characters, 1,892 natural substances, 1,109 botanical and zoological illustrations, and 11,096 historical formulas, the work stands as a landmark in pre-Linnaean taxonomy of plants, animals, and minerals. Extending this textual craft into urban heritage, century-old apothecaries such as Chen Liji (Guangzhou, 1600), Beijing Tong Ren Tang (1669), and Hu Qing Yu Tang (Hangzhou, 1874) preserve historic compounding architecture and guild ethics as cultural heritage.",
